00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.470
I know I speak for the
vast majority of Americans

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.350
when I say thoughts
and prayers to the NRA.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:14.670
Thoughts and prayers.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.810
These are troubled times for
the National Rifle Association.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.780
The nation's oldest gun
rights organization.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.420
Last year the NRA chief
executive Wayne LaPierre,

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.420
who has been at the helm of
the organization for 30 years

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.180
was accused of financial fraud

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.790
by former NRA president Oliver North.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:33.400
When North accused LaPierre
of spending donor money

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.900
on private jet travel and luxury wardrobe,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.650
LaPierre fired him and the NRA sued North.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:39.800
North countersued,

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.500
claiming that he was a whistleblower

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.370
who was booted from the organization

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.320
because he repeatedly tried to warn others

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.510
that LaPierre and other key employees

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.000
breached their fiduciary
duties to the NRA.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.610
You know it's 2020 when
Oliver frigging North

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.220
is the one who's blowing the whistle

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.810
on untoward and unscrupulous activity.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.720
Now New York has accused
LaPierre and his cronies

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.940
of funneling millions of
dollars into their own pockets

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.250
for lavish vacations,
golf club memberships.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.020
Luxury black car services,
African hunting trips,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.060
and much more.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.540
Newark attorney general
Tish James filed a lawsuit

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.510
to dissolve the NRA following
an 18 month investigation

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.440
that allegedly found evidence
of financial misconduct

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.430
that contributed to a loss
of more than $64 million

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:20.830
over a three year period.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.210
The suit alleges the top NRA executives

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.270
misused charitable
funds for personal gain.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.420
Awarded contracts to
friends and family members,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.530
and provided contracts to former employees

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:31.240
to ensure loyalty.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.370
When NRA board members
tried to blow the whistle

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.560
on this allegedly fraudulent conduct,

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.370
they allegedly faced
intimidation and retaliation.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.830
So it seems the New
York AG is out for blood

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.350
and is looking to dissolve the NRA itself.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.470
Can she do that?

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.530
Is this just a partisan political attack

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.750
on a very successful gun
rights lobbying group?

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.713
Or is there more to the story?

00:01:50.713 --> 00:01:53.463
(dramatic music)

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.090
Hey legal eagles it's time
to think like a lawyer

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.220
because New York has the
NRA in its crosshairs.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.280
See what I did there?

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.720
Now according to the State of New York,

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.427
the effect of the NRA's
internal misconduct, quote,

00:02:05.427 --> 00:02:07.630
"has been to divert
millions of dollars away

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.440
from the NRA's charitable mission,

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.860
imposing substantial
reductions in its expenditures

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.060
for core program services,
including gun safety,

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.290
education, training. member
services and public affairs."

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.760
But the NRA and its supporters
have slammed the lawsuit

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.790
as just the latest attempt by a blue state

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.350
to cancel a successful conservative group.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.120
- It's just a lawsuit to crush

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.480
an influential conservative organization

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.820
in an election year Rudy?

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:30.902
- Of course it is.

00:02:30.902 --> 00:02:33.160
I mean she's a poor excuse
for a lawyer anyway.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.530
- And if a judge dissolves
the NRA in New York,

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.900
what happens to the
organization nationally?

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.860
Well that's what we're
going to find out today.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.770
Now obviously when you talk about the NRA

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.560
it tends to get political.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.790
And this raises a couple of concerns.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.597
First, as Popehat said,

00:02:47.597 --> 00:02:49.730
"It's perfectly possible
for two things to be true

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:50.563
at the ame time.

00:02:50.563 --> 00:02:53.850
The NRA is thoroughly
corrupt and the NYAG's action

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.900
is motivated by political animus."

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.150
And I think that that is a valid concern.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.340
We'll talk about here.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.480
That there may be
improper conduct going on,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.690
but it also may be that the investigation

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.220
is slightly motivated by political animus.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.860
And that's probably not a good thing.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.180
If we support the First Amendment,

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.910
we should support it in all cases.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.200
Even if people espouse beliefs

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.040
about things that we don't agree with.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.960
And obviously a lot of progressives

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.040
don't agree with the NRA's position.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.340
But by that same token,

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.350
there's another horn to this dilemma.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.167
Which as my friend Andrew
at Opening Arguments says,

00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:28.600
"the allegations against the NRA, if true,

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.190
not their political activities,

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:32.750
support dissolution and
to give them a break

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:34.830
because of their political activities

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.320
seems profoundly wrong.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.280
And I think that these are two things

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.430
that we should always keep in mind here.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:41.970
Free speech is free speech.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.120
We should allow people
to espouse their beliefs.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.150
And we shouldn't punish
people based on those beliefs.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:48.350
But at the same time,

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:50.920
just because organization is political

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.060
is also not a reason to give them a break.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.500
This is a complicated subject

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.000
that probably deserves its own video.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.700
And we're going to try and
thread the needle here.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:03:59.920
But I think it's worth asking

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.030
that if you are on the political left

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.610
and a charity that you happen
to ascribe to like the ACLU

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:09.080
or Planned Parenthood
were accused and convicted

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.770
of this very same things that
we're talking about here,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.790
would you support the
dissolution of that charity

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.160
in that circumstance?

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:16.220
But with that in mind,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.470
let's talk about the applicable laws here.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.540
Now there are at least 1.5
million nonprofit organizations

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:22.440
in the United States.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.780
And section 501 of the U.S. tax code

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.150
outlines which types of
nonprofit organizations

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.400
may be granted a tax
exempt status by the IRS.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:31.860
This section States that organizations

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.780
are exempt from federal income taxes

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.060
if they fall under the section,

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.060
which you might've heard before, 501(c).

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:40.260
The most common title
have nonprofit designation

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:44.110
is 501(c)(3) which applies
to groups which are organized

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.860
and operated exclusively for religious,

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.360
charitable, scientific,

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.130
testing for public safety, literary,

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:50.410
or educational purposes,

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:53.150
or to foster rational
or international amateur

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:54.160
sports competition.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.310
Or for the prevention of
cruelty to children or animals.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.370
Nonprofits organized under 501(c)(3)

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.750
cannot be involved in
lobbying or politics.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.115
But organizations that
file under 501(c)(4)

00:05:04.115 --> 00:05:07.200
are permitted to engage in
some political activities

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.250
without losing their tax exempt status.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:10.870
And the NRA is organized

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.500
as a section 501(c)(4) organization.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.301
And like a section 501(c)(3),

00:05:15.301 --> 00:05:17.930
a (c)(4) organization is tax exempt

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:19.860
for federal income tax purposes.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.100
But that doesn't mean that they're exempt

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.670
from payroll taxes, real
estate taxes and so on.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.830
And in addition donations
to a (c)(4) organization

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:29.150
are typically not deductible by the donor

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:32.190
as a charitable contribution
for federal tax purposes.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.610
But that's just under the federal system.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:35.800
States also have certain regulations

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.660
and reporting requirements for 501(c)(4)s.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:40.350
So let's talk about New York State law

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.170
and the attorney general's authority

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:44.170
to dissolve a nonprofit.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.670
The NRA as a 501(c)(4) corporation

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.790
is a charity under New York State law.

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:51.660
Subject to the authority of
the New York Attorney General.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.240
And New York's not for profit law

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.210
gives the attorney general
powers over nonprofits

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:57.957
including dissolving a, quote,

00:05:57.957 --> 00:06:00.250
"corporation that has
acted beyond its capacity

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.210
Restraining any person or persons

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.610
from acting as a domestic
or foreign corporation

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:05.600
within the state."

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:06.470
Seeking a judgment,

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:09.160
removing a director of
a corporation for cause.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.850
Restraining a corporation from
undertaking any activities

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:12.683
in New York State.

00:06:12.683 --> 00:06:15.350
And compelling directors and
officers of a corporation

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:16.817
which has been dissolved to quote,

00:06:16.817 --> 00:06:20.070
"account for the assets of
the dissolved corporation."

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.790
This gives the state AG
the right two nonprofits

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:24.460
who don't comply with New York law.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.210
Barring them from operating in the state

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:27.900
and dissolving their legal charter.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.700
And requiring the directors
and officers to pay back

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:33.490
any money or assets that
were improperly diverted.

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:35.390
So how often does this happen?

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:37.640
Do the courts ever dissolve a nonprofit

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.560
after the state AG files a lawsuit?

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:40.650
Well, yes.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.570
Courts have dissolved nonprofits

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.090
when they commit serious fraud.

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:46.610
And despite the fact that
it's sort of been memory holed

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:47.443
at this point,

00:06:47.443 --> 00:06:48.830
you might actually recall

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:50.790
that the Donald J. Trump Foundation

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:54.870
is a prime example of what
happens when a New York nonprofit

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.020
violates the law.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.450
So let's talk about the State of New York

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.280
versus The Trump Foundation.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.230
The State of New York sued
the Donald J. Trump Foundation

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:05.120
and its directors, President Trump,

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:07.500
Donald Trump Jr, Ivanka
Trump and Eric Trump

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.940
for improper transactions
going back at least 10 years.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.560
The lawsuit against the
Donald J. Trump Foundation

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.100
was filed in June of 2018.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:18.190
The state said that Trump repeatedly used

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:20.270
the Foundation money for
his own personal business

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:21.410
and political interests.

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.830
A judge concluded that
Trump and his children

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.330
flagrantly breached
their fiduciary duties.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:27.960
For example, in 2016,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.730
the presidential campaign
directly distributed $500,000

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:33.980
in Foundation money at a campaign rally

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:35.740
right before the Iowa caucus.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:37.760
Then candidate Trump
subsequently took credit

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:38.760
for making those payments,

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:40.970
citing them as proof of his good deeds.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:44.160
In reality, the money that
he doled out was not his own.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.850
It was given by individual
donors to the Trump Foundation.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:51.410
By law, this money could not
be distributed by the campaign.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:52.760
After the court's decision,

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:55.010
President Trump admitted
to dozens of violations

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:55.950
of New York law,

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:57.590
including using the Foundation's funds

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:59.090
for that Iowa campaign event.

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:01.180
He also admitted that he
used the Foundation funds

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:04.230
to settle legal obligations of
companies that he controlled.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:07.130
And that the Foundation spent
money on sports memorabilia

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:08.250
and champagne,

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.600
as well as on a portrait
of Trump that cost $10,000.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.060
Trump admitted the Foundation

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:16.290
failed to provide oversight
over the Foundation

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:18.310
and failed to adopt
legally required policies

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:19.340
and procedures.

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:20.787
He admitted that these failures quote,

00:08:20.787 --> 00:08:23.210
"Contributed to the
Foundation's participation

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:25.340
in seven illegal transactions."

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:27.290
The Trump family stipulated that the AG

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:29.610
could take possession of the
charity's remaining assets

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:32.580
and that the Foundation
would be legally dissolved.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:35.510
If President Trump ever founds
another New York charity,

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:38.700
he will be subject to close
monitoring by the state.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.870
So please consider this
your friendly reminder

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:42.540
that the president of the United States

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.300
was the head of a fraudulent
charitable organization

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.390
that diverted charitable funds
away from charitable ends

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.250
like helping cancer patients,

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:54.180
and used them for his own personal gain.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:55.013
Yeah.

00:08:55.013 --> 00:08:57.590
That a thing that actually happened.

00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:59.480
All right we're gonna
talk about the allegations

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.050
against the NRA in a second.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:04.000
And believe me, they
are pretty scandalous.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.420
But first let's talk about
some of the requirements

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.990
that New York has for nonprofits.

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:11.610
New York nonprofits must
appoint directors and officers

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:14.210
who will discharge the duties
of their respective positions

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:15.043
in good faith.

00:09:15.043 --> 00:09:17.350
And with the care an
ordinarily prudent person

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:18.950
in a like position would exercise

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:20.770
under similar circumstances.

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:22.507
These individuals may only be paid quote,

00:09:22.507 --> 00:09:25.470
"a reasonable amount for
services actually rendered."

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:27.410
They cannot be paid from dividends

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:28.617
or from distributing quote,

00:09:28.617 --> 00:09:31.870
"any part of the organization's
income or profit."

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:33.710
The board of directors must also adopt,

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:35.760
implement, and assure compliance

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.800
with the conflict of interest policy.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:40.060
It must implement a policy
protecting whistleblowers

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:41.350
from retaliation.

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:43.990
Some nonprofits must
register and file accurate

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:45.810
and complete reports with
the attorney general.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:48.330
Executives cannot lie or
make misstatements of fact

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.680
in any financial statement
that is filed with the state.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.200
And the New York Prudent Management

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:53.540
of Institutional Funds Act

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.760
governs the management
and investment of funds

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:59.280
held by not-for-profit corporations
and other institutions.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:00.360
The NRA does in fact,

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:03.270
hold such institutional funds
and the board of directors

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.580
has a duty to use such funds
for the good of the company

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:08.240
and not for themselves.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.150
So let's talk about the allegations

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:11.950
of the actual lawsuit itself.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:13.950
Despite being a national organization,

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:16.810
the NRA was chartered by
special act of the state

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:20.620
of the New York Legislature
on November 17th, 1871.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:21.580
And throughout its history,

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:23.810
it's been legally domiciled
in the State of New York.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:26.850
Despite having its headquarters
in Fairfax, Virginia.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:29.320
The NRA has members and
engages in fundraising

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:32.030
throughout the United States
and has five key missions.

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:34.190
One, to protect and defend
the Second Amendment.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:36.650
To promote public safety
and law and order.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:39.520
To train people on safe
handling of small arms.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:40.720
To promote shooting sports

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:43.500
and to promote and defend hunting.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:44.360
And interestingly,

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:47.370
the NRA of today looks really nothing

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:49.870
like the NRA of 50 years ago.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:52.140
Frank Smyth, the author of "The NRA,

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.900
The Unauthorized History"

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:55.990
explains that the
current makeup of the NRA

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:59.510
relates back to the
1977 Cincinnati Revolt.

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:00.343
At which time,

00:11:00.343 --> 00:11:03.940
hardcore gun rights advocates
essentially launched a coup

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:06.380
against the current management of the NRA

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:09.740
and took over the ideological
structure of the organization.

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:12.100
And at the same time that
the members of the NRA

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:13.960
who launched the Cincinnati Revolt

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:15.710
and made the organization tack

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:18.020
to a much more ideologically
extreme positions

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.070
with much more local activity.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:21.730
They also at the same time

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:24.360
prevented any kind of
public financial disclosures

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:25.650
from being released.

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:28.360
Which had been standard
practice up until that time.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:29.340
So with that in mind,

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:32.260
the New York AG's lawsuit
alleges that Wayne LaPierre

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:33.500
and the executive staff

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:35.650
have effectively looted the NRA's funds.

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:38.110
Diverting the organization
from furthering the goals

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.510
listed by their mission statement.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:42.260
For personal gain unrelated
to their core missions.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:44.060
Information from several whistleblowers

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:45.180
is included in the complaint.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:47.490
Including two members of the NRA board

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:50.000
described as dissident
one and dissident two.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.970
Describe the numerous efforts by LaPierre

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:54.370
to block them from their legal obligations

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:55.680
to fiduciaries.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.340
The New York lawsuit names the
NRA as a corporate defendant,

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.800
and also the top four
officials at the NRA,

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:02.110
including Wayne LaPierre.

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:04.500
Former chief financial
officer, Woody Phillips.

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:06.400
Former chief of staff, Joshua Powell.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:09.880
And general counsel, John
Frazer as individual defendants.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:12.250
The suit accuses LaPierre
of using charitable funds

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.700
for personal gain including
a post employment contract

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:16.760
valued at more than $17 million.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:19.180
It was not approved by the
NRA's board of directors.

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:22.450
LaPierre allegedly received
more than $1.2 million

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:24.220
in expense reimbursements over four years.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:25.750
Including gifts for friends,

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:28.840
travel expenses and memberships
at golf clubs and hotels.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:30.840
LaPierre allegedly used money for purposes

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:32.220
unrelated to the NRA's mission.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:34.770
Such as sending extended
family on private vacations

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:36.030
paid for by the NRA,

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:37.380
even when he was not present.

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:40.400
Traveling to Africa with
his wife on a Safari

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:42.020
gifted by an NRA vendor.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:46.070
Spending at least $3.6 million
on luxury black car services

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:48.370
and travel consultants in just two years.

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:49.590
And in the past five years,

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:51.860
LaPierre and his family have
really visited the Bahamas

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.310
by private air charter
at least eight times.

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:57.360
At a cost of more than
$500,000 to the NRA.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:59.530
Although NRA employees are required to use

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:01.653
the NRA's in-house travel
agent to book travel.

00:13:01.653 --> 00:13:04.880
LaPierre's travel was handled
by an outside travel agent

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:08.630
who from 2016 to 2019 was paid a fixed fee

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.360
of $19,000 a month.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:14.400
And this kind of expense
obviously adds up fast.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.210
Now the complaint alleges that LaPierre

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:18.830
used a secret poison pill contract

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:21.580
to make him the chief
executive officer for life.

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:23.640
And a contract allegedly requires LaPierre

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:26.180
to receive an annual payment of $1 million

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:27.880
if he chooses to retire.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:29.810
However, if he's ousted from the group,

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:32.560
the contract actually
pays LaPierre even more,

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:35.350
up to $1.5 million annually.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:36.350
According to the lawsuit,

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:39.310
neither the NRA's first
nor second vice president

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:41.990
signed the contract as
required by NRA policy

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:43.450
governing procurement.

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:45.210
And there's no evidence that the NRA board

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:47.430
was even aware of the original contract

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:49.450
or its numerous extensions.

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:50.680
In addition to Oliver North,

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:53.160
several other people tried to
blow the whistle on LaPierre

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:55.980
but he apparently retaliated
against those individuals.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.840
The suit also alleges that
LaPierre hired other defendants

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:01.150
in order to help him
further his self-dealing.

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:03.840
The New York AG says that
Pierre hired Woody Phillips,

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:05.230
Joshua Powell and John Frazer

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:07.240
despite their lack of skills or experience

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:09.510
for their respective roles
and responsibilities.

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:10.960
At Lafayette discretion,

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:12.740
Phillips allegedly instituted a practice

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:14.680
whereby millions of
dollars in entertainment

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:17.310
and travel expenses
incurred by NRA executives

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:19.360
were billed to the NRA as disbursements

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:20.920
by the NRA's largest vendor.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:23.670
That practice evaded both
the NRA's own accounting

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:26.060
and board established expense
reimbursement process.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:29.280
And the IRS requirements for
proper expense reimbursement.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:30.410
And according to the lawsuit,

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:31.750
LaPierre, Phillips and Powell

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:33.800
regularly use this
pass-through arrangement

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:36.170
to conceal private travel and other costs

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:37.810
that were largely personal in nature.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:41.030
And wasting substantial
charitable resources.

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:43.300
The lawsuit also alleges
that Woody Phillips,

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:45.030
who as treasurer was responsible

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:47.370
for managing the financial
operations of the NRA,

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.500
lied on financial disclosure forms

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:51.630
and set up numerous
deals to enrich himself

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:52.600
and his girlfriend.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:54.040
And according to the lawsuit,

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.130
several of Phillip's staff
became whistleblowers

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:57.350
in the summer of 2018.

00:14:57.350 --> 00:14:59.610
Disclosing to the NRA audit committee,

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:02.350
longstanding failures by
NRA senior executives,

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:03.960
including Phillips and Powell,

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:07.140
to comply with NRA financial
policies and procedures.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.740
And Phillips allegedly set
up a contract for himself

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:11.150
just before he retired,

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:13.700
that was supposed to be for
ongoing consulting services

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:15.000
to the incoming treasurer.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.210
But the incoming treasurer
told the New York AG

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:19.630
that he was not even
aware of this contract,

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:21.330
and that the contract was never approved

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:23.010
by the NRA board of directors.

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:24.220
The lawsuit also alleges

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:27.480
that the former NRA Chief of
Staff Joshua Powell's salary

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:29.980
more than tripled a
little more than two years

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:31.110
into his tenure,

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:32.910
which started in 2016.

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:35.700
His salary started at $250,000,

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.700
but rose to $800,000

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:39.750
by the second anniversary with the group.

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:41.220
And although LaPierre described Powell

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:43.110
as abusive to his employees,

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:46.140
Powell was promoted after
his abuses were discovered.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.650
And Powell was also accused
of directing charitable funds

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:50.660
to be used for the benefit of his family.

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:54.250
Including approving a $5
million consulting contract

00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:56.160
with the firm McKenna and Associates.

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:58.699
Which in turn that consulting organization

00:15:58.699 --> 00:16:00.700
ultimately hired Powell's wife

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.980
and paid her $30,000 a
month in consulting fees

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:05.260
through the NRA.

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:06.420
And even before this lawsuit,

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:09.050
the NRA actually fired
Powell in January of 2020

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.020
for misappropriating funds.

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:13.630
And the complaint alleges
that NRA General Counsel

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:16.150
John Frazer didn't even
meet the requirements

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:20.080
required for in-house
attorneys for a nonprofit.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:22.130
And despite the lack of qualifications,

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:24.450
he was simply hired to help LaPierre

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:26.457
funnel money to himself and others.

00:16:26.457 --> 00:16:28.949
And that Frazer is accused of
repeatedly certifying false

00:16:28.949 --> 00:16:31.480
or misleading annual
statements by the NRA.

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:34.270
Now the lawsuit identifies
18 formal causes of action

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:35.103
against the defense.

00:16:35.103 --> 00:16:35.936
In other words,

00:16:35.936 --> 00:16:38.700
things that give rise to
some violation of law.

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:40.840
And the key claim against
the NRA as a corporation

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:42.580
is that it has carried on, conducted,

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:43.850
or transacted it's business

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:46.450
in a persistently fraudulent
or illegal manner.

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:49.340
Or by abuse of its powers
contrary to public policy.

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.840
And the lawsuit seeks the
dissolution of the NRA

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:53.600
as a nonprofit in the state.

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:55.570
And the second cause of
action against the corporation

00:16:55.570 --> 00:16:58.330
cites New York State law that
allows judicial dissolution

00:16:58.330 --> 00:16:59.233
of the nonprofit where the directors

00:16:59.233 --> 00:17:02.577
or members in control of
the corporation have quote,

00:17:02.577 --> 00:17:04.960
"looted or wasted the corporate assets,

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:06.370
have perpetuated the corporation

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:08.030
solely for their personal benefit,

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:10.320
or have otherwise acted in an illegal,

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:12.080
oppressive or fraudulent manner."

00:17:12.080 --> 00:17:14.100
And if this relief is granted,

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:16.300
the state seeks to
distribute the NRA's assets

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:18.830
towards causes consistent
with its mission statement.

00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:21.130
And LaPierre, Phillips, Frazer and Powell

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:23.100
are sued in their individual capacities

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:24.580
for injustly enriching themselves

00:17:24.580 --> 00:17:26.090
because they obtained a benefit

00:17:26.090 --> 00:17:27.640
that should have gone to the NRA.

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:30.200
And the New York AG
asked that all of the men

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:32.410
repay the compensation and reimbursements

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:34.530
that they received at the
detriment of the company.

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:36.740
The men are also being
sued for their breaches

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:39.640
of their fiduciary and
professional duties to the NRA.

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:41.240
And are sought to be barred

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:43.150
from operating charities of the future.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:45.240
And this leads to one of
the main counterarguments

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:47.630
about the way that the New
York AG has handled this

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:51.020
and actually seeking the
dissolution of the NRA itself.

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:53.830
And pointing to why it might
be politically motivated.

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:56.470
There've been a fair number
of people that have said that

00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:58.300
if these allegations are true,

00:17:58.300 --> 00:17:59.960
the top of officials at the NRA

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:01.680
should obviously be prosecuted

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:04.740
and forced to disgorge the
profit that they received

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:06.400
at the detriment of the company.

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:08.250
But even if that's the case,

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.540
the organization should in fact remain.

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:13.460
Even if we may disagree with
what that organization does.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:15.050
Unlike the Trump Foundation,

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:17.390
which existed apparently solely

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:20.450
to benefit the members of the Foundation.

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:22.580
And to be able to divert charitable funds

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:24.180
towards their own aims,

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:26.070
the NRA does a number of things

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:29.300
that fit with the mission
of the organization itself.

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:30.950
Even if some of those funds

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:33.750
were diverted by the men at the top.

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:35.370
But the counter-counterargument

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:37.550
is that that's an incredibly low bar

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:38.670
and that these allegations

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:41.210
do actually warrant
dissolving the company itself.

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:44.470
But this is not the only
lawsuit against the NRA.

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:46.100
Washington, D.C.'s attorney general

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:47.790
is also suing the NRA Foundation,

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:49.400
which is a separate nonprofit

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:50.870
that's chartered in the district.

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:53.110
For allegedly diverting funds to the NRA

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:57.130
to help pay for improper
spending by top NRA executives.

00:18:57.130 --> 00:18:57.963
And as a result,

00:18:57.963 --> 00:19:00.160
the DC lawsuit is demanding
a constructive trust

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:02.740
be placed over the amount it
says was improperly diverted

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:04.050
to the main NRA.

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:07.190
And the NRA is also battling
its former advertising agency,

00:19:07.190 --> 00:19:08.430
Ackerman McQueen.

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:10.720
The ad agency allegedly had
an arrangement with the NRA

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:12.930
to help them cover up the excess spending.

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:17.190
And the lawsuit against
Oliver North is also ongoing.

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:20.620
So if New York dissolves the
NRA and removes its officers,

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:22.080
what happens next?

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:25.670
Well the NRA would cease to
exist in the State of New York.

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:27.950
But nothing would stop it
from chartering under the laws

00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:29.380
of a more hospitable state.

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:30.930
But if the court grants the state's motion

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:32.900
to seize and redistribute
the NRA's assets,

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:35.100
the organization would
probably be crippled.

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:36.770
Even if they went to another state.

00:19:36.770 --> 00:19:39.380
The New York lawsuit seeks
to remove the core officers

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:40.213
of the NRA,

00:19:40.213 --> 00:19:42.100
which would likely mean that
a new version of the NRA

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:44.260
would need a different leadership group.

00:19:44.260 --> 00:19:46.120
But given the popularity
of the NRA's mission

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:46.980
in the United States,

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:49.130
it's likely that many other organizations

00:19:49.130 --> 00:19:50.890
would probably step into the vacuum

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:54.500
left by the disappearance
of the NRA would take over.

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:57.170
And many justified the mission of the NRA

00:19:57.170 --> 00:19:59.780
and the Second Amendment as
a way to protect their family

00:19:59.780 --> 00:20:00.840
and their homes.

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:02.270
But contrary to popular belief,

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:04.810
the second amendment only
allows you to possess a firearm.

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:07.410
It doesn't absolve you from
the liability of using it.

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:09.860
And if you did shoot an
intruder in your own home,

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:12.850
you're almost be certainly
subject to a massive lawsuit.

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:16.120
A better way would be to stop
a crime before it happens.

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:18.470
Imagine if you had 24
hour video surveillance

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:20.140
and guards that could stop a package thief

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:22.120
or an intruder before they got in.

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:24.040
Well with Deep Sentinel, you can.

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:25.700
And I can tell you from
personal experience

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:26.720
that it's amazing.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:28.810
I personally reached out
to them because I love it.

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:30.290
I actually just moved houses

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:32.290
and I knew I wanted a new security system.

00:20:32.290 --> 00:20:33.760
Because I used to live in a house

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:35.350
with a traditional security system

00:20:35.350 --> 00:20:37.100
with one of those stupid key pads

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:38.610
where the alarm would go off

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:40.260
if you didn't put your code in in time.

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:43.700
And I actually got fined hundreds
of dollars multiple times

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:45.970
when the police were
called out unnecessarily.

00:20:45.970 --> 00:20:48.740
And I also tried one of those
systems with wifi cameras.

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:50.400
And all it did was record the fact

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:52.130
that somebody stole my package.

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:53.960
But Deep Sentinel is different.

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:55.840
Deep Sentinel is the only security company

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:58.350
that actually monitors
your property in real time

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:01.590
and will actually intervene
before something happens.

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:03.350
Once the security system is triggered

00:21:03.350 --> 00:21:05.780
their surveillance guards,
actual human beings,

